e.\n- Interview notes, store manager Dorian Pask, 3 Nov (manager-account): Pask does not think self-checkout is the issue. He believes shoplifting has grown since the Dunmere bus interchange opened beside the car park on 16 February. Door-counter footfall averaged 14,100 visits a week in Q1, 15,200 in Q2 and 15,900 in Q3. He cites recorded shoplifting incidents in the store log: 6 in Q1, 11 in Q2 and 15 in Q3, mostly razors, cosmetics and batteries. 'My team is logging more than they ever have. We need a guard on the door.' Asked whether staff theft could be involved, he said he trusts his team and pointed to the ethics-line case being closed.\n- Ethics-line case 26-0871 and asset protection follow-up (log): Received 21 Aug, anonymous caller: 'The night stocker at Dunmere [name redacted] keeps taking boxes out the back door after close. Saw it on the 12th and the 19th.' Follow-up by AP investigator R. Okafor, 2 Sep: Reviewed rear-door and dock CCTV for 12 and 19 August, 22:00–06:00. On both nights the named stocker made two trips through the rear door carrying flattened cardboard bales to the compactor, as listed on the closing checklist. No merchandise or sealed cartons seen leaving. Night supervisor confirms bale runs are assigned to this employee. Case closed: unsubstantiated.\n- Store 14 interim shrink report, Q1–Q3 FY2026 (financial-record): Shrink as % of department sales, Q1 / Q2 / Q3. Grocery 0.8 / 1.1 / 1.3. Fresh 1.6 / 2.0 / 2.4. Health & beauty 2.9 / 3.6 / 4.4. General merchandise 2.0 / 2.5 / 3.1. Store total 1.5 / 1.9 / 2.3. Each department's share of store sales stayed within one point across all three quarters: grocery 46%, fresh 24%, health & beauty 14%, general merchandise 16%. Footnote: Q1–Q3 figures are interim estimates extrapolated from rotating department cycle counts. The annual full physical count, carried out by the independent count contractor, is scheduled for 7 December.\n- Email from Bram Castellane, Regional Operations Manager, 6 Nov (manager-account): Re: December count, store 14. Given holiday staffing pressure, I propose we take store 14 out of the 7 December full physical count and roll forward the Q3 book figure for year-end. We already know where that store's number is heading, and the count costs about 140 staff hours plus contractor fees. The other 37 stores would count as planned. Please confirm by 20 November so the contractor can re-plan its schedule.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe rise at store 14 may not be real. The Q1–Q3 figures are estimates from partial cycle counts, with an error band of about ±0.6 points a quarter. In past years, about half of two-quarter rises like this one were not confirmed by the year-end full count. Store 14's own full-count shrink was 1.5–1.8% in FY2023–25. The incident log is also unreliable as a trend signal: a chain-wide app launched on 1 April, and recorded incidents rose chain-wide while shrink stayed flat. Neighbouring stores 9 and 22 show no common pattern. Department sales mix barely moved, so a mix shift doesn't explain it. The self-checkout lanes, customer theft and staff theft all remain possible, and none is established. The ethics-line tip was checked on CCTV and showed bale runs, not theft. What to do: keep store 14 in the 7 December full count and reject the proposal to drop it. That count is the cheapest way to find out whether the loss is real. Don't spend on a guard, pull the lanes, or suspend the stocker until the count is compared with the interim figures. If the loss is confirmed, run a targeted review by department and item type. I'm moderately sure of this, and the main gap is that I haven't seen item-level or receiving data.\n","numLines":19,"startLine":1,"totalLines":19}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015d5KnVQqXMGBN7tfF9XSDZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
